 ## Overview
-Röttger et al. Rendering is a technique that mitigates the high computational cost of volume integration by approximating the rendering integral and moving the computation to a pre-process [S1, book]. It computes a 3D lookup table of colors and opacities parameterized by front scalar value, back scalar value, and distance, which is stored in a 3D texture for hardware-accelerated lookups during rendering [S1, book][S2, unofficial]. This method builds on [AR mapping to 3D texture](884-ar-mapping-to-3d-texture.md) by using the 3D texture to evaluate the volume composition operator directly via texture interpolation [S4, unofficial]. It is distinguished from standard ray casting by replacing per-voxel integration with texture lookups, and from pure pre-classification by supporting arbitrary transfer functions including isosurface effects through the parameterization of the lookup table [S2, unofficial].
+Röttger et al. Rendering is a technique that mitigates the high computational cost of volume integration by approximating the rendering integral and moving the computation to a pre-process [S1, book]. It computes a 3D lookup table of colors and opacities parameterized by front scalar value, back scalar value, and distance, which is stored in a 3D texture for hardware-accelerated lookups during rendering [S1, book][S2, unofficial]. This method builds on [AR mapping to 3D texture](884-ar-mapping-to-3d-texture.md) by storing pre-computed integration results in texture memory to avoid per-voxel evaluation [S1, book]. It is distinguished from standard ray casting by replacing per-step integration with texture lookups, and from pure pre-classification by supporting arbitrary transfer functions including isosurface effects through the parameterization of the lookup table [S2, unofficial].
 
